.TH DISREPL 1
.SH NAME
disrepl \- write Dis programs at a prompt
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B disrepl
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Disrepl
reads Dis instructions one at a time and runs the program they build up,
printing eight scratch words after every line.
.PP
Nothing here interprets Dis.
.I Emu
already interprets and compiles it,
.IR asm (1)
assembles it and
.IR disdump (1)
disassembles it; what this adds is the loop, and somewhere for a program
to keep its state from one line to the next.
.PP
It does that by keeping every line typed and running the whole program
again after each one.
The consequence is worth knowing before it surprises anyone: a line with
an effect outside the virtual machine happens again on every subsequent
line.
What it buys is that the frame, its type descriptor and the module's
link section all come from a wrapper the Limbo compiler generated, so
the garbage collector is told the truth about which words hold pointers
without this program having to work a descriptor out for itself.
.SS Scratch
Eight words are free for use and are printed after each run:
.IP
.EX
s0  80(fp)      s4  120(fp)
s1  96(fp)      s5  128(fp)
s2  104(fp)     s6  136(fp)
s3  112(fp)     s7  144(fp)
.EE
.PP
They are not contiguous, and the gaps are not spare.
.B 88(fp)
is the argument area the print is built in,
.B 152(fp)
holds a pointer to the
.I Sys
module, and anything from
.B 160(fp)
up is the frame of that call.
Writing to those breaks the program in ways that have nothing to do with
what was typed.
.SS Labels
A branch target is a bare label name:
.IP
.EX
movw $5,96(fp)
top: addw 80(fp),96(fp),80(fp)
subw $1,96(fp),96(fp)
bnew 96(fp),$0,top
.EE
.PP
which leaves 15 in
.BR s0 .
Writing
.B $top
instead does not fail.
It assembles, as the immediate 0, and branches to the first instruction
of the module \(em so the program restarts forever.
This is worth stating because it is what the first loop written here
did, and the assembler gives no warning.
.SS Commands
.TP
.B .list
Show the program so far.
.TP
.B .drop
Remove the last instruction and run again.
.TP
.B .clear
Start again.
.TP
.B .asm
Show the assembly that would be handed to
.IR asm (1),
wrapper and all.
.TP
.B .dump
Disassemble the module that was built.
.TP
.B !
Stop a program that is still running.
.TP
.B .quit
Leave.
.SS Stopping a program
Typing
.B !
while a program is running stops it.
There is a reason it is a command rather than a keystroke: on this
platform
.I emu
wires the host interrupt signal to a clean exit, so the usual interrupt
character would take the whole emulator down and the session with it.
The interrupt is therefore a line of input, which arrives on the same
channel as everything else and is selected against the running program
in one
.BR alt .
.PP
Input that arrives while a program is running and is not
.B !
is queued rather than acted on, which is what lets a session be piped in
without its next command being mistaken for an interrupt.
.PP
A program that runs for more than three seconds is stopped anyway, for
when nobody is watching.
A line that does not assemble, or that has to be stopped, is not kept: a
mistake would otherwise repeat on every line after it.
A line that assembles and runs but raises is kept, because it is a real
instruction doing a real thing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /tmp/disrepl.s
the assembly of the current session
.TP
.B /tmp/disrepl.dis
the module built from it
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/disrepl.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR asm (1),
.IR disdump (1),
.IR limbo (1),
.IR prog (3)
.PP
``The Dis Virtual Machine'' in Volume 2.
.SH BUGS
Only the eight scratch words can be seen.
There is no way to look at module data, the heap, or a frame word that
is not one of the eight, and no way to print anything from a program
under construction \(em the printing is the wrapper's, not the user's.
.PP
Re-running the whole session after each line is quadratic in the length
of the session, and repeats external effects.
The alternative, a live frame that persists between lines, needs the
type descriptor to grow with it; see
.B doc/hpc-plan.md
for the argument.
.PP
The wrapper is fixed text.
Changing the number of scratch words means regenerating it with
.I limbo
.B -S
and editing the source, and the procedure for that is a comment rather
than a program.
.PP
There is no history, no editing beyond what the terminal provides, and
no way to load a session from a file or save one to it.
